  "query_preview": "../Monday through Thursday/...A low pressure system is\nexpected to move into the Gulf, sending a front through the \nnortheast gulf coast late Sunday night that will shift \nsoutheastward over the northern and central panhandle through \nMonday, reaching the southern panhandle by Tuesday morning. Strong\nupper-level flow will amplify rainfall accumulation and duration,\nwith high confidence that many areas will see 1.5 to 2.5 inches \nof rain through Tuesday. The heaviest totals are anticipated along\nthe gulf coast from Baranof Island northward and along windward \nmountains, while southern panhandle areas at/near sea level will \nlikely see lower amounts between 1 and 1.25 inches.\n\nWinds will increase as the front approaches, though the strongest \nspeeds will remain offshore in the Gulf. Most inner channels are \nexpected to reach at least a fresh southeasterly breeze, with Lynn \nCanal potentially seeing a strong breeze through midday Monday \nbefore speeds decrease Tuesday afternoon and shift northerly as the \nlow tracks south. Midweek, building high pressure will drive \nprecipitation out of the region and bring clearing skies to the \nnorthern and central panhandle. Temperatures will generally persist \nin the upper 50s and 60s, though Wednesday\u2019s clearing will likely \npush daily highs slightly warmer.",
